THE COMPARISON THAT DECIDES EVERYTHING
================================================================================
  one appended token embedding row, ||e_v|| = 1.0000  (std = 1/sqrt(D))
  ||X0|| over 12 tokens                     = 3.5002
  so a single token changes the block input by 28.6%

  delta* (5% tolerance) = 4.394e-01
  one-token perturbation = 1.000e+00
  ratio = 2.28
  => a SINGLE TOKEN INSERTION EXCEEDS THE LINEARISATION RADIUS by this
     factor. The Hermitian linear model does not describe the block even
     across one token boundary, so it cannot be the substrate for a
     per-token conservation law. It is at best a sub-token approximation.

================================================================================
SPARSE (SINGLE-ROW) PERTURBATION -- what appending/altering one token really is
================================================================================

  The isotropic sweep above asks the wrong question. A token does not perturb the
  whole residual stream uniformly; it perturbs ONE row of it. Appending a token
  changes T and so is not a perturbation of the fixed-T map at all -- the honest
  object is the sparse update: replace one row by another embedding. Radius may
  differ sharply between dense and sparse directions, and only the sparse one is
  load-bearing for a per-token conservation law.

